# Venue record — NeurIPS 2026, Main Track

Sources: `ht-role venue neurips` (retrieved 2026-11-14 by the world's venue library; every requirement below carries its source there), the Reviewer Guidelines page https://neurips.cc/Conferences/2026/ReviewerGuidelines (read 2026-11-25), and the official style kit copied unmodified into `template/` (`neurips_2026.sty`, `neurips_2026.tex`, `checklist.tex`; kit declares itself the January 2026 style). Anything not established is marked **not established**.

## 1. Choice of venue and why
The objective asks for one of ICML, ICLR, NeurIPS, the latest edition, with the venue's paper checklist.

| | NeurIPS 2026 (chosen) | ICLR 2027 | ICML 2027 |
|---|---|---|---|
| main text | 9 pages, **one column**, 5.5 in text width | 9 pages, one column, 5.5 in | 8 pages, **two columns**, 6.75 in |
| refs / appendix / checklist | do not count | refs, appendix, AI-use statement do not count | refs, impact statement, appendix, AI statement do not count |
| required checklist | **yes, 16 items, in the PDF** (desk reject without) | no checklist (AI-use statement; reproducibility statement optional) | no checklist (impact statement + AI disclosure) |
| newest edition? | newest NeurIPS call; `neurips.cc/Conferences/2027/CallForPapers` is HTTP 404 (re-checked 2026-11-25) | 2027 call published; under review | 2027 call published; submission opens 2027-01-08; organisers announce process changes "by late November" (not seen yet) |

Reason: the objective's own example of a "checklist the venue asks to submit with the paper" is the NeurIPS checklist, which maps onto what the objective grades (reproducibility, statistical significance, compute, limitations, licences). Single column with a 9-page body suits the outline's figure/table density (many small-multiple panels). "Latest edition" is read as the newest edition of that conference that has a published call and style kit; NeurIPS 2027 does not exist yet. The NeurIPS 2026 deadlines have passed: the paper is built to this edition's rules, not submitted (the release goes to the world's user).
Risk accepted: ICLR 2027/ICML 2027 are calendar-newer. If the user prefers one of them, `ht-role venue iclr|icml --template DIR` has the kits; the figure widths below would change (ICML two-column).

## 2. What the paper must satisfy (checkable)
**Length.** Main text ≤ **9 content pages including all figures and tables**. Not counted: references, acknowledgments, technical appendices, the paper checklist. Over the limit → "will not be reviewed". Camera-ready would allow a 10th page (not relevant here: no camera-ready is produced). No page limit on references/appendix/checklist; the paper must stand alone without the appendix (reviewers need not read it).
**Template.** Use `template/neurips_2026.sty` unmodified, `\usepackage{neurips_2026}` with **no option** (= submission mode: anonymous author block, line numbers, "Submitted to NeurIPS 2026" footer). Do not edit margins, fonts, `\textwidth`, or load `fullpage`/`geometry`; "tweaking the style files may be grounds for desk rejection". US Letter. Only Type 1 / embedded TrueType fonts in the PDF (kit text, `neurips_2026.tex` l.376: "must only contain Type 1 or Embedded TrueType fonts"). Correction of an earlier statement of mine: with `\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}` the style's own `\texttt` (anonymous e-mail line, and any `\texttt` in the paper) is set in a bitmap Type 3 font on this TeX Live 2022 (no cm-super); checked by compiling the style with `fontenc` alone and reading `pdffonts`. Fix without touching the style: `\usepackage{courier}` after it, which gave Type 1 only in the same test. `final` only for a camera-ready, `preprint` if a non-anonymous public version is wanted; neither changes the page limit.
**Anonymity (submission mode).** No author names/affiliations, no acknowledgments (the `ack` environment is hidden automatically), own work cited in third person, no identifying URLs; code and data links must be anonymous. Do not refer to the line numbers.
**Required parts.** (a) Paper checklist, 16 items (Claims; Limitations; Theory; Reproducibility; Open access to data and code; Experimental setting; Statistical significance; Compute resources; Code of ethics; Broader impacts; Safeguards; Licences for existing assets; New assets; Crowdsourcing/human subjects; IRB; LLM usage). Rules: answer `\answerYes{}`/`\answerNo{}`/`\answerNA{}` with a 1–2 sentence justification pointing to sections; delete the instruction block but keep the heading "NeurIPS Paper Checklist"; do not modify the questions; place it after references and appendix. A filled copy belongs under `paper/` (the kit's `template/checklist.tex` stays unmodified); `\input` it after the appendix. "No" is acceptable with a justification. (b) LLM use must be described in the experimental setup when "important, original, or non-standard" in the approach, and answered in checklist item 16; routine editing/code assistance needs no documentation. (c) No "broader impacts" section is required; any societal-impact discussion must fit in the page limit.
**Supplementary.** Text appendices go in the same PDF after references (no separate appendix PDF). Code/data: one anonymised ZIP ≤ 100 MB with training and evaluation code and a dependency specification, "ideally self-contained and executable". PDF ≤ 50 MB. **Not established:** NeurIPS 2026 does not fix a reproduction-README format; the objective's list (versions, data steps, seeds, commands, expected time, which script makes which table/figure) is the standard here, and it lives in `code/` (analysis).
**Reproducibility/ethics facts for the checklist.** Datasets are public (German Credit CC BY 4.0 per UCI; ACS PUMS public Census data, no separate licence text found by the task; folktables MIT; OpenML "Public"). The checklist asks to name licences of existing assets (item 12).

## 3. How reviewers judge (NeurIPS 2026 review form)
Quality, Clarity, Significance, Originality, each 1–4; Overall 1–6 (6 Strong Accept … 1 Strong Reject; 4 Borderline accept, 3 Borderline reject); Confidence 1–5; Questions (ideally 3–5); Limitations. Definitions: **Quality** — technically sound, claims supported, methods appropriate, complete work, authors candid about strengths and weaknesses; **Clarity** — clearly written, well organised, "enough information for an expert reader to reproduce its results"; **Significance** — impactful for the community, others will use or build on it, offers unique data/conclusions; **Originality** — new insights, deepened understanding, or important properties of existing methods count equally with a new method ("novel insights from evaluating existing methods"), differences from prior work are clear, novel metrics or framings count.
**Contribution type is chosen by the authors at submission and cannot be changed.** This paper is an empirical evaluation → **General** ("most submissions"). **Negative Results** is a separate type with a *higher* significance and originality bar ("unexpected or surprising", "must not be merely an experiment that failed", should contradict a widely held belief). Consequence for the story: do not frame the paper as a Negative Results paper, even if the answer is "unreliable"; the study's contribution (protocol, conditions map) is judged as General, and the risky single-run-check result (R1) can fail inside it. Reviewers may use the checklist, but a "No" answer is "typically not grounds for rejection"; minor formatting overruns are not to be penalised.
Review scores of this venue's accepted papers are not available to the world; no acceptance threshold is claimed.

## 4. Layout numbers for figures and tables (for `presentation`)
From `neurips_2026.sty` / `.tex` (single column):
- Text block **5.5 in × 9 in** (396 pt wide); left margin 1.5 in, top margin 1 in. **Figures and tables must fit the text block**; place with `\includegraphics[width=<fraction>\linewidth]{...}`, never beyond margins. There is no two-column spanning; one placed figure ≤ 5.5 in wide. A figure is 9 in tall at most (a full page of figure counts against the 9 pages).
- Body font Times (`ptm`) 10 pt on 11 pt; sans-serif Helvetica (`phv`). Match figure text to Times or Helvetica (embed fonts as Type 1/TrueType; no Type 3: set `pdf.fonttype=42` in matplotlib).
- Floor sizes enforced by the style: `\footnotesize` = 8 pt, `\scriptsize` = 7 pt, `\tiny` = 6 pt; references may be `\small` (9 pt). **The venue states no minimum for text inside figures**; it asks that "all artwork must be neat, clean, and legible" with dark lines, and that captions and body remain legible printed in black/white or colour. Recommended floor, my reading (not a venue rule): **7 pt at placed size** for tick labels and legends, 8 pt for axis labels and table text, since the style itself never prints text below 6 pt and body footnotes use 8 pt. `presentation` placed 7 pt as a placeholder; it is consistent with this.
- Captions: figure caption **below** the figure, table caption **above** the table; lower case except first word and proper nouns; the kit's sample captions ask to "explain what it shows and add a key take-away message". Tables: `booktabs`, centred, **no vertical rules**. Caption skips are fixed by the style (7 pt).
- Colour is allowed; keep figures legible in greyscale (distinguish by marker/linestyle as well as colour).
- Figures are numbered consecutively; sub-panels are one figure.

## 5. How accepted papers of this audience are written (measured sample)
Sample: **13** main-track papers on feature-attribution/SHAP estimation, explanation reliability and evaluation, tabular models, predictive multiplicity — NeurIPS 2017–2023 (8), ICML 2020/2022 (3), ICLR 2022/2025 (2), listed in `accepted_papers/sample.csv`; venue checked on Semantic Scholar venue field; all main track. Counted by `accepted_papers/analyze.py`, `refs.py`, `intro.py` on the **arXiv PDF** (which can differ from the camera-ready: one paper (Dasgupta et al.) is an extended version, two are post-camera-ready revisions; counts of main-body items stop at the first "References" heading). Selection is mine, **not** a random draw of the venue; treat the numbers as an indication of a range. The reserved paper was not searched for or used.

| quantity | min | median | max | notes |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| figures in main text | 1 | **4** | 7 | sub-panels count as one |
| tables in main text | 0 | **1** | 6 | |
| figures + tables | 2 | **5** | 9 | the outline's plan (5 + 3 = 8) is at the top of this range |
| abstract words | 87 | **171** (IQR 111–202) | 240 | one paragraph |
| introduction words (11 parsed) | 295 | **704** (IQR 516–889) | 1282 | ≈ 1 page at 5.5 in; 2020+: median 559 |
| bibliography entries | 11 | **45** (IQR 38–50) | 68 | 2020+ median 48; the oldest (2017) has 11 |
| main body | ≈ 8–9 pages in their own formats | | | this venue: ≤ 9 |

Writing pattern observed (I read the abstracts of Slack et al. 2021, Musco & Witter 2025, Han et al. 2022, Ghalebikesabi et al. 2021 and the opening of two introductions; the counts come from the scripts; the rest of the sample was not read closely):
- Abstract: one paragraph: stakes ("explanations are used in high-stakes settings") → the gap/limitation of existing methods → what the paper does → the headline finding, sometimes with one number (Leverage SHAP: "approximately 50% reduction in error vs the SHAP library") → scope. Empirical-evaluation abstracts state what was compared and what held or did not; the sample's abstracts seldom state a numeric result beyond one or two.
- Introduction: context and use case; the concrete limitation, often with a small motivating example; "In this paper…"; a contribution paragraph or bullet list (7 of the 11 parsed introductions use the word "contribution", 3 use bullets); 5 of 11 refer to a figure in the introduction; parsed citation counts in the introductions range 0–19 (median 6; the parser is crude, so only the order of magnitude is claimed).
- Related work is usually a section of its own, 0.5–1 page, organised by thread (estimators; disagreement/robustness; evaluation) with a closing sentence on how this paper differs.
- Limitations appear in the discussion or conclusion; NeurIPS asks for them in checklist item 2.
- None of the sample's main texts has more than 9 figures + tables (maximum 9: Gorishniy et al., 3 + 6).

## 6. Budget of the 9 pages (recommendation; the outline decides content)
Abstract + Introduction ≈ 1.3 p (abstract ≤ 200 words, introduction ≈ 650–750 words + Fig. 1); Related work ≈ 0.6 p (≈ 40–50 references in total are typical, the bibliography does not count); Setting and protocol ≈ 1.4 p; Experimental design ≈ 1 p (with Table 1); Results ≈ 3.7 p; Discussion and limitations ≈ 0.7 p; checklist, references, appendices outside. Figures/tables in the body: **at most 6 figures + 3 tables, fewer is the norm (median 5 items)**. Planning estimate, not measured: a full-width (5.5 in) multi-panel figure takes 2.5–3.5 in of height, so 5 figures + 3 tables ≈ 4–4.5 of the 9 pages and would leave the Results prose about 2 pages — the outline's cut order (§5 of the outline) will be needed unless figures are set at ≈ 0.5 page. Anything cut goes to the appendix, which may hold the full sensitivity grids (outline), but "if material is critical to the evaluation it must be in the body".

**Check against Outline rev 2 (2026-11-25).** The rev-2 body list is Fig 1–5, Tables 1–3 and a Fig 6 panel attached to Table 3, i.e. 5–6 figures + 3 tables = 8–9 items. The sample's maximum is 9 items and its median 5 (§5), so this is at the top of what accepted papers hold; it is within the venue's rules (no item limit, only the 9 pages), so the limit is pages, not count. Planning estimate (not measured): Tables 1–3 at about 0.25–0.4 page each plus 5 figures at about 0.3–0.4 page leave ≈ 4.5–5 pages for text; this is enough only if Results prose stays near 2.5 pages. Recommendation: keep the outline's cut order (Fig 1 → appendix first, then Table 3/Fig 6, then merge Fig 5 into Fig 4), and decide it from the compiled page count in `paper-repo`'s document build, not from this estimate. The B-sensitivity (A2) and the sensitivity grid (A1) in the appendix are allowed, but NeurIPS 2026 says material critical to evaluation belongs in the body: the headline's scope sentence ("B=50 random rows") must therefore be in the abstract/§4, not only in A2.

